# Netlib LP standard set — pinned instances and reference optima.
#
# Instance files never enter this repository (PLAN 2.10). This manifest is
# what pins them: bench/fetch.sh downloads each one and refuses any whose
# sha256 does not match the line below.
#
# Source of the instances: the dataset published alongside Thorsten Koch,
# "The Final NETLIB-LP Results" (ZIB-Report 03-05), at
# https://www.zib.de/koch/perplex/data/netlib/mps/ — plain MPS, gzipped.
# The checksum is of the .mps.gz exactly as that server returns it.
#
# rows/cols are what JAOS must load: the constraint rows without the
# objective row, and the structural columns. Both were cross-checked
# against the canonical netlib PROBLEM SUMMARY TABLE, which counts the
# objective row among its rows — they agree on every instance but boeing1,
# where netlib carries one more free row than Koch's file does.
#
# reference is the optimal objective. Where source is "koch" it is the
# nearest double to the exact rational Koch proved with rational
# arithmetic. Where it is "netlib" it comes from the netlib readme, whose
# values were computed with MINOS 5.3 and are not exact: Koch found eight
# of them wrong by more than the gate's tolerance (80bau3b, ganges,
# greenbea, greenbeb, nesm, pilot, pilot-we, scrs8), which is why his
# values are preferred wherever they exist.
#
# The two lines still sourced "netlib" are a known defect, not a gap in
# Koch's results: his tables cover maros-r7 and pilot87 like everything
# else. His printed value for pilot87 is about 301.71034733 against the
# 301.71072827 below, a difference of 3.8e-4 where the gate's tolerance on
# that instance is 3.0e-4 — so pilot87 is being judged against a reference
# outside tolerance of the exact optimum. The verdict does not change
# either way, but the reference is wrong. Fixing it needs the exact
# rationals, which were published at zib.de/koch/perplex and are no longer
# reachable there; reading them off the report PDF reproduced only 23 of
# the 92 values already known, so it is not a source to set ground truth
# from. See bench/README.md.
#
# objconst is the objective constant JAOS applies from the file's RHS entry
# on the objective row, under the CPLEX convention the reader implements and
# docs/format-support.md records. Both published reference sets omit it, so
# the gate compares against reference + objconst. It is nonzero on exactly
# one instance of this set — e226 — and that is the only place the two
# conventions are distinguishable, since grow7, grow15 and grow22 carry the
# same entry with a value of zero.
#
# name        sha256                                                            rows   cols  reference                 source  objconst
